================================================================================
5. FINDINGS
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING A — NON-BLOCKING. Three of the five no-turn call sites have no
regression test: reverting `condemnHarness`, `unknown harness` or `harness
unavailable` individually to a `nowIso` literal leaves the suite green.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Mutants S2, S3, S4 above. The class is closed by CONSTRUCTION (§1) — the shipped
code routes all five through noTurnProbe, and mutants M5/M6 pin the shared
helper's own contract, so an edit to noTurnProbe cannot pass silently. What is
unpinned is a future edit that reintroduces a literal at one of those three
sites specifically.

Why this is not blocking:
  - no present defect: §1's enumeration and §4's live run both show the invariant
    holding at every site, including condemnHarness;
  - the brief requires a positive control per BEHAVIOUR, not per call site, and
    no behaviour enumerates the five sites;
  - the two sites that ARE pinned (S1 mark-before-spend, S5 no-control) plus
    M5/M6 cover the shared root, which is where a regression would actually land;
  - a third round for a change that cannot alter behaviour is worse than the
    exposure it removes.

Worth noting sharply, though: S4's site — condemnHarness — is the exact path
round 1's PROOF A and PROOF C both used. The site the finding came from is now
verified by construction and by §4's live run, but not by a unit test. The cheap
close is to parameterize behaviour 9(iii)'s per-pair case over the
control-failure path as well as the no-control path: the same seeded vintage row,
a manifest WITH a codex seat whose control fails, asserting the same two
preservations. A handful of lines against fixtures that already exist. RECORDED
FOR THE RETRO as the general class — "a fix that collapses N call sites into one
helper should pin the helper AND leave at least one behavioural case per
collapsed site, or say why not" — rather than held against this opus.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE — the NEVER_PROBED_AT sentinel writes "" into a field typed as an ISO date.
Accepted, reasoned, no action.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`HarnessProbe.at` is non-optional, so a pair with no evidence time must carry
SOME string. "" is the honest choice and is self-consistent with the brief's own
reader rule ("an unparseable `probes[].at` is treated as never probed"), which is
also what `priorAtMs` implements. It adds no state, which the brief demanded
("no cursor and no new state"). No shipped consumer is misled: store.ts ignores
`at` and `probes` entirely, and /api/models never sees either. The alternative —
a fabricated old date — is the one the brief refuses by name. Recorded so the
choice is visible rather than discovered.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CARRIED FROM ROUND 1, unchanged and still not this opus's work
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The vendor-spend sentinel class (round-1 deviation-2 ruling): nothing in the repo
mechanically prevents the next verb's tests from spending real money. The
builder's near-miss was caught by a sandbox log nobody is obliged to read, and
the fix is injection discipline — a memory, which CLAUDE.md's own standing rule
forbids as enforcement. My §2 sentinel run is a working ~6-line prototype of the
guard. STILL FILED FOR THE RETRO as its own check-rule or test-infra opus.

Round-1 observations (a) per-battery clock granularity, (b) the disagreement
warning re-printing stale disagreements, (c) a targeted run's control spending a
turn before being marked, (d) `probe` discarding total_cost_usd from the claude
envelope: all still true, all still non-blocking, none regressed. (a) is now
harmless for ordering because a no-turn row no longer takes the run clock.

